WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.113
- [Narrator] The ground begins to tremble.

00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:04.946
- [Announcer] Three.

00:00:04.946 --> 00:00:07.525
- [Narrator] Massive engines roar to life.

00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:08.800
- [Announcer] Two.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.103
- [Narrator] Billowing clouds of exhaust.

00:00:11.103 --> 00:00:11.936
- [Announcer] One.

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:13.560
- [Narrator] And then a
blinding pillar of fire.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.860
- [Announcer] Liftoff. We have a liftoff.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.090
- [Narrator] A mighty voyager
leaves the Earth behind

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.973
to explore the vast
universe among the stars.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:30.860
Launching a rocket into space

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.903
is one of humankind's
crowning achievements.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.980
Although they come in many
different shapes and sizes,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.453
all rockets are propelled by
engines that produce thrust.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.660
The rockets that launch into space

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.600
are made up of four major
systems, based on function.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.220
The structural system makes up the frame

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.850
that holds the rocket together

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.560
and consists of the cylindrical
body, nose cone, and fins.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.730
Next, the propulsion system

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.690
takes up the most amount of space

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:06.870
and includes the rocket
engine, fuel, and oxidizer.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.730
The payload system depends on each mission

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.290
and consists of anything a
rocket is carrying into space,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.383
like a spacecraft,
satellite, or human being.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.580
Lastly, the guidance
system is made up of radars

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.630
and computers that provide
stability for the rocket

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.623
and control maneuvers in flight.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.540
In order to launch into space,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.670
all four of these rocket
systems must work together

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.270
to overcome the force of gravity.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.150
The launch begins when the
rocket's propulsion system

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.630
starts to generate a
massive amount of thrust.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.260
Thrust is the force
produced by burning fuel

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.670
as exhaust gases escape
through the engine.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.140
Once the rocket generates more
thrust than its own weight,

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.910
it lifts into the air to
begin its powered ascent.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.700
During this phase of the flight,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.230
the weight of the rocket
will constantly change

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.940
as fuel continues to burn off.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.620
As a result, most spacebound rockets

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.000
use a technique called staging

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.720
to reduce dead weight
and increase efficiency.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.360
The method involves
breaking off a large rocket

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.780
into two or three smaller rockets

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.360
that fall away at different
stages of the launch.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.500
As the rocket continues into orbit,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.700
its sophisticated guidance
system maintains balance

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.330
and steers to keep the
flight trajectory on track.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.520
At the correct altitude and speed,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.760
the upper stage engine cuts off,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.340
completing the rocket's journey

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.413
from Earth's surface into orbit.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.400
Long before blasting into space,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.350
rockets were used here on Earth

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.200
as early as the 13th century.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.950
The first known rockets were
introduced by the Chinese

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.400
in 1232 A.D.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.590
These fire arrows were used to
fight against invading armies

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.970
and were made by attaching
fireworks packed with gunpowder

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.637
to long arrows.

00:02:58.637 --> 00:03:02.500
By the 16th century, the
use of rockets for amusement

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.380
had spread from Asia to Europe,

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.880
where they gained popularity
in elaborate firework displays

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.163
at celebrations and festivities.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.360
During the following centuries,
the work of scientists,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.830
like Isaac Newton and his Laws of Motion,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.790
began to greatly increase knowledge

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:24.200
into the forces behind rocketry
and how to control them.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.640
And by the end of the 18th century,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.000
military forces around
the world began to apply

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.450
these new scientific
understandings to the battlefield,

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.880
transforming the earlier, crude rockets

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.853
into powerful weapons of war.

00:03:39.429 --> 00:03:42.720
However, the true dawn of space rocketry

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.300
began in the early 20th century,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.700
thanks to massive
technological improvements

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.003
in rocket science and aeronautics.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:56.170
By the 1950s, the stage was
set for the modern space age,

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.470
and development began

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.860
on sophisticated launch vehicle systems

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.460
like the Atlas rocket family,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.320
which launched America's
first astronaut into orbit,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.650
the Titan rockets,

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.840
which were behind the
pivotal Gemini missions

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.500
during the Space Race, and
the Saturn rocket family,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.680
which includes the largest
and most powerful rocket

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.623
ever successfully launched,
the mighty Saturn V.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.020
Standing as high as a 36-story building

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.670
and weighing more than 3,000 tons,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.510
this behemoth was used to launch

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.430
the Apollo missions to the moon.

00:04:32.384 --> 00:04:36.084
- We choose to go to the moon
not because they are easy,

00:04:36.084 --> 00:04:37.500
but because they are hard.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.950
- [Narrator] Since the
beginning of human history,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.410
adventurers have looked at the skies

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.890
and dreamt of touching the stars.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.610
And today, innovations in rocketry

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.910
are opening up possibilities
to launch astronauts

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.050
farther into space than ever before.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:05:00.050
Whether our sights are set
on the moon, Mars, or beyond,

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.590
the future of rocketry
and space exploration

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.577
is only just blasting off.

